(Q9) How can you log the output of a script using the tee command?
The tee command is used to display output on the screen and write it to a file at the same time.
SYNTAX:
your-command | tee logfile.txt
This writes the output of your-command to logfile.txt and displays it on the screen.

(Q10) Explain with an example how shell scripting can automate system
administration tasks.

Task
Description
Backups
Automatically back up files or databases
Log rotation
Archive and clear old logs
User management
Create/delete users in bulk
Monitoring
Check disk usage, CPU load, or service status
Software updates
Automate apt/yum updates
Scheduled jobs
Run with cron at specific times

EXAMPLE:

#!/bin/bash
# Script: disk_monitor.sh
# Purpose: Monitor disk usage and send a warning if above threshold

THRESHOLD=80

# Get disk usage for root partition
USAGE=$(df / | awk 'NR==2 {print $5}' | sed 's/%//')

# Compare with threshold
if [ "$USAGE" -ge "$THRESHOLD" ]; then
    echo "WARNING: Disk usage is at ${USAGE}%!" | tee -a /var/log/disk_usage_alert.log
    # Optionally send email or notify admin
else
    echo "Disk usage is normal: ${USAGE}%"
fi
